  "answer": "**Earlier application papers:** The supplied passages do not answer whether the other side can obtain papers from the earlier application or examination file. I cannot establish that entitlement from these extracts. The Manual does describe the Registrar giving the applicant the opponent’s evidence in support, but that is not an answer about access to earlier application papers (TMM/Part51/1/1/2).\n\n**Late evidence — Manual practice:** Yes, it may be considered, but not routinely. The general rule is that evidence filed out of time is not considered. The filing party must pay the relevant fee and make a compelling case for its consideration (TMM/Part51/2/2/5~1).\n\nThe late evidence and supporting submissions must be filed on Objective Connect. The Registrar notifies the other party; no decision on consideration is made until the evidence stage has ended and at least one party has asked to be heard. If the delegate finds a compelling case, the other party can make representations before the delegate decides whether to consider the information (TMM/Part51/2/2/5~2).\n\n**Legislation:** These late-evidence statements describe Manual practice, not legislation. The supplied Act and Regulations passages do not directly answer either question."
